use std
into fit.amni

Imports: os, gfx, math, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.codec_png, gfx.motion, gfx.scene3d and json.

This program measures how closely a Helium render of a character matches a target picture (an OpenMOSS render on a plain light studio background) and is the first stage of the image-to-model fit: it fits the camera to the target's silhouette, renders the character over the target's background, and reports silhouette overlap, structural similarity and color difference, with a side-by-side picture and a difference heat map.

Module state: `var tw = 0`, `var th = 0` (target size), `var tpx byte[>..]` (target RGBA), `var tmask f32[>..]` (target coverage 0 or 1 per pixel), `var rpx byte[>..]` (render RGBA at target size, composited over the background), `var rmask f32[>..]` (render coverage per pixel, 0..1), `var bg = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }` (the target background in 0..255 per channel), `var cam_eye = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }`, `var cam_look = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }` (the fitted camera), `var hit_img int[>..]`, `var hit_u f32[>..]`, `var hit_v f32[>..]`, `var zb f32[>..]`, `var pal gfx::mat4[>..]`, `var fast = false` (render once instead of three times while searching), `var pose_n int[>..]` (nodes the pose fit turned), `var pose_a f32[>..]` (their angles), `var img_changed int[>..]`, `var key_k = 1.0`, `var fill_k = 1.0`, `var amb_k = 1.0`, `var sun_az = 0.0`, `var sun_el = 0.0` (light fit factors), `var item = -1`, `var asset = -1`, `var big = gfx::Texture {}` and `var fov = 0.5`.

`num(s const byte[:]) f32` parses one argument or token as a real and returns it `as f32`.

`at(px const byte[:], i int, c int) f32` is `px[i * 4 + c] as f32`.

`gray_bg()` finds the target background and its mask. The background is the per-channel median of the pixels on the picture's border rows and columns (collect them in three f32 lists, sort each with the standard sort, take the middle element) stored in `bg`. Then flood fill from every border pixel (an int stack of pixel indices and a byte list `seen` of tw * th): a pixel joins the background when its largest channel difference from the pixel it was reached from is at most 6, its largest channel difference from `bg` is at most 40, and its saturation (largest channel minus smallest) is at most 18. `tmask[i]` is 0 for background pixels and 1 for every other pixel.

`load_pose(path const byte[:])` reads a pose file with the unqualified `read_file(path, data)` into a `byte[>..]` (return when it fails). Each non-empty line is a node name followed by ten reals separated by single spaces: translation x y z, rotation quaternion x y z w, scale x y z. For each line find the node with `gltf::find_node(asset, name)` and, when it is not negative, call `s3d::set_node(item, node, float3 { tx, ty, tz }, linalg::Quat { qx, qy, qz, qw }, float3 { sx, sy, sz })`.

`shot(eye float3, look float3)` renders the character at twice the target size and downsamples it: set `s3d::view` to an `s3d::Camera` from eye to look, up (0, 1, 0), fov_y `fov`, near 0.05, far 100; call `s3d::render(big)` once when `fast`, else three times; `px = gfx::read_pixels(big)`. A big pixel is empty when its red, green and blue are all 0. Fill `rmask` and `rpx` for each target pixel (x, y) from the four big pixels (2x + u, 2y + v) for u, v in 0..1: coverage is the share of non-empty ones (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 or 1); each output channel c is the mean over the four of (the big pixel's channel when it is non-empty, else `bg` channel c), rounded and clamped to 0..255; alpha 255.

`bbox(m const f32[:]) int, int, f32` returns the first row with any pixel of m at least 0.5, the last such row, and the mean column of those pixels (as f32); returns 0, 0, 0 when there are none.

`lab(r f32, g f32, b f32) float3` converts an sRGB color in 0..255 to CIE L*a*b* (D65): linearize each channel `c / 255` with the sRGB curve, convert to XYZ with the standard sRGB matrix, divide by the white point (0.95047, 1.0, 1.08883), apply `f(t) = t^(1/3)` when t > 0.008856 else `7.787 t + 16 / 116`, then L = 116 fy - 16, a = 500 (fx - fy), b = 200 (fy - fz).

`de2000(p float3, q float3) f32` is the CIEDE2000 color difference between two Lab colors with kL = kC = kH = 1, following Sharma, Wu and Dalal (2005) exactly: C* means, the G factor with 25^7, a' scaling, C' and h' (degrees in 0..360, 0 when a' and b' are both 0), ΔL', ΔC', Δh' with the ±180 wrap, ΔH', the means L', C' and h' with the hue-mean rule, T, Δθ, R_C, S_L, S_C, S_H, R_T, and the square root of the sum.

`metrics(heat byte[>..]&) f32, f32, f32, f32` compares `tpx`/`tmask` with `rpx`/`rmask` and fills `heat` (tw * th * 4 bytes). IoU: intersection is the sum of `min(tmask, rmask)`, union the sum of `max(tmask, rmask)`. Color: over the pixels where `max(tmask[i], rmask[i]) > 0` take `d = de2000(lab of the target pixel, lab of the render pixel)`, sum it and count; the heat pixel is black when d is 0 rising to red at d = 10 and to white at d = 20 and above (red = min(d / 10, 1) * 255, green and blue = clamp((d - 10) / 10, 0, 1) * 255), and for the other pixels the heat pixel is the target pixel at a quarter brightness. Structural similarity on luminance `0.299 r + 0.587 g + 0.114 b` over the union mask's bounding box (rows and columns where the union is above 0): for every window of 7 by 7 pixels whose top-left corner steps by 2 in each direction inside that box, compute the means, variances and covariance of target and render luminance and `((2 mx my + C1)(2 cxy + C2)) / ((mx² + my² + C1)(vx + vy + C2))` with C1 = (0.01 · 255)² and C2 = (0.03 · 255)², and average those windows that hold at least one union pixel. Return IoU, SSIM, mean ΔE (0 when no pixels counted) and the share of counted pixels with ΔE below 3.

`raster()` finds, for each target pixel, which texture spot of the character it shows, using the final fitted camera and the current pose. Call `s3d::pose(item)` first. Clear `hit_img` (int, tw * th, -1), `hit_u`, `hit_v` (f32, tw * th) and a depth list `zb` (f32, tw * th, 1e30). `vp = gfx::perspective(fov, tw as f32 / th as f32, 0.05, 100.0) * gfx::look_at(cam_eye, cam_look, float3 { 0.0, 1.0, 0.0 })`. For every node n of the asset (from `gltf::assets[asset].nodes.first`, count `.nodes.count`) with `gltf::nodes[n].mesh >= 0`: when its `skin >= 0` fill `pal` with `motion::joints(gltf::nodes[n].skin, pal)`. For every primitive of the mesh whose material has a base texture (`m = gltf::materials[prim.material]`, `m.base_tex.texture >= 0`, `img = gltf::textures[m.base_tex.texture].image >= 0`): for each vertex v (floats at `(prim.vfirst + v) * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`), its world position is, when the node is skinned, the sum over all four joints c = 0, 1, 2 and 3 (four terms) of `weight[22 + c] * linalg::point(pal[joint[18 + c] as int], position)`, else `linalg::point(motion::world[n], position)`; project it with vp to clip space, then to pixel coordinates `px = (ndc.x * 0.5 + 0.5) * tw`, `py = (0.5 - ndc.y * 0.5) * th` and depth `ndc.z`, keeping uv0 (floats 6, 7). Skip triangles with any vertex behind the camera (clip w <= 0). Rasterize each triangle (indices `gltf::indices[prim.ifirst + 3 t + k]`, local to the primitive, both windings) over its pixel bounding box clamped to the picture, testing pixel centers with edge functions and barycentric weights; where the interpolated depth is below `zb`, interpolate uv, and when the base texture's alpha at that uv (see `texel`) is at least 128 store depth, `img`, u and v.

`texel(img int, u f32, v f32) int` is the byte offset into `gltf::pixels` of the texel at uv (u, v) of image img: `im = gltf::images[img]`, `x = clamp(floor(fract(u) * im.width), 0, im.width - 1)`, `y = clamp(floor(fract(v) * im.height), 0, im.height - 1)`, offset `im.pixels.first + (y * im.width + x) * 4`.

`lin(c f32) f32` turns an sRGB channel in 0..255 into linear 0..1 and `enc(l f32) f32` turns linear 0..1 back into 0..255 (both with the exact sRGB curve).

`paint()` corrects the character's base textures so the render moves toward the target. Call `raster()`. For each image that has hits keep a correction grid of `ceil(width / 4)` by `ceil(height / 4)` cells with three f32 sums and a count (allocate grids lazily, one per image, in flat lists with per-image offsets). For every pixel i with `tmask[i] == 1`, `rmask[i] == 1` and `hit_img[i] >= 0`: for each channel the ratio is `clamp((lin(target) + 0.004) / (lin(render) + 0.004), 0.25, 4.0)`, added to the image's cell at `(floor(fract(u) * width / 4), floor(fract(v) * height / 4))`. Then for every cell with a count, and every texel of that 4 by 4 block, multiply each linear texel channel by `pow(mean ratio, 0.8)`, clamp to 0..1 and store it back encoded into `gltf::pixels` (keep alpha). Re-upload every changed image: `gfx::update_texture(s3d::tex_srgb[img], gltf::image_pixels(img))` and `gfx::generate_mips(s3d::tex_srgb[img])` when `s3d::tex_srgb[img].id != 0`. Return the number of cells changed.

`turn(node int, ang f32)` turns one joint about the camera's viewing axis (world z) by `ang` radians on top of its rest pose: with `n = gltf::nodes[node]`, `p = n.parent`, the axis in the parent's frame is `linalg::unit3(linalg::qrotate(qconj(mquat(motion::world[p])), float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 1.0 }))` when p >= 0 (else world z), and the joint is set with `s3d::set_node(item, node, n.t, linalg::qnorm(linalg::qmul(linalg::qaxis(axis, ang), n.r)), n.s)`. Record it in `pose_n`/`pose_a` (replace the angle when the node is already listed). `mquat(m gfx::mat4) linalg::Quat` is the rotation of a matrix whose columns may be scaled (normalize the three columns, then the standard matrix-to-quaternion conversion) and `qconj(q)` the conjugate.

`score() f32` renders with `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)` and returns the silhouette IoU (intersection over union of `tmask` and `rmask`, as in `metrics`).

`pose_fit()` matches the limbs to the target silhouette by coordinate search. `fast = true`. Joints, in order: upperarm_l, upperarm_r, lowerarm_l, lowerarm_r, thigh_l, thigh_r, calf_l, calf_r (skip names `gltf::find_node` cannot find). Call `s3d::pose(item)` once so `motion::world` is current. Two passes; in each pass, for each joint with current angle `a0` (0 at first) and best score `best = score()`: for the step sizes 0.28, 0.14, 0.07, 0.035 and 0.014 radians, try `a0 + step` and `a0 - step` with `turn`; keep whichever raises the score (update `a0` and `best`), else restore `turn(node, a0)`. `fast = false`. Print "pose " plus each joint's name and angle (4 decimals).

`save_pose(path const byte[:])` writes the turned joints as a pose file in the same format `load_pose` reads (name, translation, rotation, scale of the override as set by `turn`, one line each) with the unqualified `write_file`.

`set_lights()` applies the light fit factors: `s3d::sun = s3d::Sun { to_sun: linalg::unit3(float3 { 0.35 + sun_az, 0.8 + sun_el, 1.0 }), rgb: float3 { 2.2 * key_k, 2.2 * key_k, 2.2 * key_k }, shadows: true, shadow_far: 10.0, map_size: 2048 }`; `s3d::lights` cleared and `s3d::add_light(float3 { -14.0, 7.0, 14.0 }, float3 { 260.0 * fill_k, 260.0 * fill_k, 260.0 * fill_k }, 1000.0)`; `s3d::sky.ambient = amb_k`.

`tone() f32` renders with `shot(cam_eye, cam_look)` and returns the mean CIEDE2000 difference over pixels where both `tmask` and `rmask` are 1 (1000 when there are none).

`light_fit()` lowers that difference by coordinate search: `fast = true`; three passes over the factors key_k, fill_k, amb_k (multiplied or divided by 1.25 then 1.1) and sun_az, sun_el (plus or minus 0.2 then 0.07): try each change with `set_lights()` and `tone()`, keep it when the difference drops, else undo it. `fast = false`; call `set_lights()`. Print "light key " key_k " fill " fill_k " amb " amb_k " az " sun_az " el " sun_el (4 decimals).

`emit(d json::Doc&, n int, out byte[>..]&)` writes JSON node n back as compact JSON text using the json module's formatters (`json::format_key`, `json::format_string`, `json::format_int`, `json::format_number`, `json::format_bool`, `json::format_null`): objects with their members in order, arrays with their elements, strings, whole numbers as ints, other numbers as numbers.

`export_glb(src const byte[:], out const byte[:], prefix const byte[:])` writes a copy of the source .glb whose changed images are the painted ones. Read src with `read_file`; the JSON chunk is at byte 20 with length `u32le(12)`, the binary chunk follows (its length at the 4 bytes after the JSON chunk, its data 8 bytes later). Parse the JSON with `json::parse`. New binary: the old data, then for each image k of the asset (global image `gltf::assets[asset].images.first + k`) with `img_changed` set, the bytes of the file `prefix + "_tex" + global index + ".png"` (already saved) padded with zeros to a multiple of 4, remembering offset and length. New JSON: emit the root object member by member; for "bufferViews" emit the old elements and then one `{"buffer":0,"byteOffset":o,"byteLength":l}` per new image; for "images" emit each element as is except changed ones, which become `{"bufferView":v,"mimeType":"image/png","name":<old name or "">}` with v the new view's index; for "buffers" emit `[{"byteLength":<new binary length>}]`; everything else as is. Pad the JSON with spaces to a multiple of 4. Write "glTF", version 2, total length, the JSON chunk (length, "JSON") and the binary chunk (length, "BIN" and a zero byte), all little-endian, with `write_file`. Print "glb " plus the path.

`main()`: with `a = args()`, `a[1]` is the .glb, `a[2]` the target .png, `a[3]` the output prefix, and `a[4]` an optional pose file. Read the target with the unqualified `read_file` and decode it with `codec_png::decode(data, tpx)` into `tw`, `th` (print "target failed" and return when it fails). Resize `tmask` and `rmask` to tw * th and call `gray_bg()`. Open headless with `gfx::open_headless(tw, th)` (on failure print "gpu: skipped (no device: " plus `gfx::error()` plus ")" and return). Load the glb with `gltf::load` into `asset` (negative: print "load failed: " plus `gltf::error()` and return), `item = s3d::add(asset)`, `s3d::place(item, gfx::mat4_identity)`, and when `a.len > 4` call `load_pose(a[4])`. Light it as a soft light-gray photo studio: `s3d::sun = s3d::Sun { to_sun: linalg::unit3(float3 { 0.35, 0.8, 1.0 }), rgb: float3 { 2.2, 2.2, 2.2 }, shadows: true, shadow_far: 10.0, map_size: 2048 }`; `s3d::add_light(float3 { -14.0, 7.0, 14.0 }, float3 { 260.0, 260.0, 260.0 }, 1000.0)`; `s3d::sky = s3d::Sky { zenith: float3 { 0.6, 0.6, 0.6 }, horizon: float3 { 0.55, 0.55, 0.55 }, ground: float3 { 0.3, 0.3, 0.3 }, ambient: 1.0, draw: false }`; `s3d::look.bloom = 0.0`; `s3d::look.desaturate = 0.0`; `s3d::look.curve = 1`. `s3d::init(2 * tw, 2 * th)` and `big = gfx::render_target(2 * tw, 2 * th, gfx::RGBA8)`.
Fit the camera to the target silhouette. With `ty0, ty1, tcx = bbox(tmask)` (print "empty target" and return when ty1 <= ty0): start with `d = 3.2`, `cy = 0.85`, `cx = 0.0`. Repeat 7 times: `shot(float3 { cx, cy, d }, float3 { cx, cy, 0.0 })`, `my0, my1, mcx = bbox(rmask)` (print "empty render" and return when my1 <= my0); `wpp = 2 d tan(fov / 2) / th`; `cy -= (((my0 + my1) as f32 - (ty0 + ty1) as f32) / 2) * wpp`; `cx += (mcx - tcx) * wpp`; `d *= (my1 - my0) as f32 / (ty1 - ty0) as f32`. Store the final camera in `cam_eye` (cx, cy, d) and `cam_look` (cx, cy, 0). Unless the environment variable `FIT_POSE` is "0", run `pose_fit()`, then repeat the camera fit loop 3 more times from the current camera (storing it again), and `save_pose(a[3] + "_pose.txt")`. Unless `FIT_LIGHT` is "0", run `light_fit()`. Render once more, then `iou, ssim, de, good = metrics(heat)` and print the line below with the prefix "fit". Then, unless the environment variable `FIT_PAINT` is "0", repeat 4 times: `paint()`, render at the final camera, recompute the metrics and print the same line with the prefix "paint" plus the round number; then save every image that paint changed as `a[3] + "_tex" + index + ".png"` with `gfx::save_shot` (RGBA, width and height of the image, pixels from `gltf::image_pixels`), and when any were saved call `export_glb(a[1], a[3] + ".glb", a[3])`.
Save three pictures with `gfx::save_shot` (each a `gfx::Shot { w, h, pf: 376840196, px }`): `a[3] + "_render.png"` (rpx), `a[3] + "_heat.png"` (heat) and `a[3] + "_side.png"`, which is target, render and heat side by side (3 tw wide, th tall). The printed line is: "<prefix> iou " iou " ssim " ssim " de " de " good " good " cam " cx " " cy " " d, each number with 4 decimals.
